## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128 bit manipulations and zero edge cases without branching.

## Key Concepts
- Branchless bit tricks
- 128 bit layout as hi and lo
- De Bruijn based ctz for 64 and 32 bit values
- Handling zero input gracefully

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline int ctz64(uint64_t x) {
  if (x == 0) return 64;
  static const int tab[64] = { /* 64 entries */ };
  uint64_t y = x & -x;
  return tab[(y * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a89ULL) >> 58];
}

int lsbIndex128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo, hi = x.hi;
  int a = ctz64(lo);
  int b = 64 + ctz64(hi);
  int m = - (int)(lo != 0);
  int res = (a & m) | (b & ~m);
  return (lo | hi) == 0 ? -1 : res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if 128 bit intrinsics are unavailable
- Extend to msbIndex128 with branchless patterns

## Why This Is Asked
Tests branchless thinking and lane-wise masking, fundamental techniques in low-level libraries and SIMD operations.

## Key Concepts
- Lane-wise masking without conditionals using two's complement arithmetic
- 128-bit composition from 32-bit chunks
- Bitwise extraction and packing operations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode C-like implementation demonstrating the core idea
uint128 blendMask128(uint128 x){
  uint32_t a3 = (x.hi >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint32_t a2 = x.hi & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint32_t a1 = (x.lo >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint32_t a0 = x.lo & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint64_t m3 = -(uint64_t)(a3 & 1);
  uint64_t m2 = -(uint64_t)(a2 & 1);
  uint64_t m1 = -(uint64_t)(a1 & 1);
  uint64_t m0 = -(uint64_t)(a0 & 1);
  uint128 result;
  result.hi = (m3 << 32) | m2;
  result.lo = (m1 << 32) | m0;
  return result;
}
```

## Quick Tests
- Input: 0x123456789ABCDEF0 → Output: 0x0000000000000000 (all lanes even)
- Input: 0x12345679_9ABCDEF1 → Output: 0xFFFFFFFF_00000000 (only A0 odd)

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128-bit data as four independent 32-bit lanes and perform lane-wise rotation without loops. Demonstrates precise bit-slicing, correct boundary handling, and minimal, provable code paths.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit packing into 4×32 lanes
- Lane-wise rotation using (x<<n) | (x>>(32-n))
- Bit masking to confine intermediate results to 32-bit lanes
- Clamping n with & 31 to handle n >= 32

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64 lo; uint64 hi; } uint128;

uint128 rotateWithinLanes128(uint128 x, unsigned n) {
    n &= 31;
    uint32 a = (uint32)(x.lo);
    uint32 b = (uint32)(x.lo >> 32);
    uint32 c = (uint32)(x.hi);
    uint32 d = (uint32)(x.hi >> 32);

    x.lo = ((uint64)((a << n) | (a >> (32 - n))) << 32) |
           ((b << n) | (b >> (32 - n)));
    x.hi = ((uint64)((c << n) | (c >> (32 - n))) << 32) |
           ((d << n) | (d >> (32 - n)));
    return x;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to derive circular adjacency via rotation, and to implement a 128-bit popcount using SWAR style reductions; ensures understanding of cross-boundary bit relationships.

## Key Concepts

- Circular adjacency using ROTL
- 128-bit representation as two 64-bit halves
- SWAR/popcount with bitwise masks and shifts

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint128 rotl128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = (x.hi << 1) | (x.lo >> 63);
  r.lo = (x.lo << 1) | (x.hi >> 63);
  return r;
}

static inline unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x){
  uint64_t a = x.hi;
  uint64_t b = x.lo;
  a = a - ((a >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  a = (a & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((a >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  a = (a + (a >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  unsigned int ca = (a * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;

  b = b - ((b >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  b = (b & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((b >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  b = (b + (b >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  unsigned int cb = (b * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;

  return ca + cb;
}
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to non-power-of-two widths?
- What are performance implications on vectorized lanes?

## Why This Is Asked

Explores a new angle: 128-bit popcount via per-half 64-bit parallel counting using a fixed, loopless sequence of masks.

## Key Concepts

- Bit-twiddling for popcount
- Handling 128-bit values by splitting into 64-bit halves
- No branches or loops; purely arithmetic masks

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint32_t popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

uint32_t popcount128(uint128 x) {
  return popcount64(x.lo) + popcount64(x.hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for 256-bit using the same pattern? 
- What are the trade-offs of inlining vs. a dedicated intrinsic on your compiler/architecture?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests depth in bit twiddling and constant-time operations; parity computation is fundamental in checksums, error detection, cryptography, and performance-critical code paths.

## Key Concepts
- Parity reduction across word boundaries by folding upper and lower halves
- Fixed-time parallel reduction using shifts and XOR
- No branches; constant-time execution regardless of input

## Code Example
```c
unsigned int parity128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t p = x.hi ^ x.lo;
  p ^= p >> 32;
  p ^= p >> 16;
  p ^= p >> 8;
  p ^= p >> 4;
  p ^= p >> 2;
  p ^= p >> 1;
  return p & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to arbitrary bit widths or for streaming parity computation?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question tests advanced bit manipulation skills. A palindrome check requires full 128-bit reversal and a single branchless comparison. It assesses understanding of endianness, cross-lane bit operations, and how to compose a complex operation from a reusable primitive.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit bit reversal (SWAR-style steps)
- Branchless comparison via bitwise XOR
- Endian-agnostic bit ordering across two 64-bit lanes

## Code Example
```c
int isBitPalindrome128(uint128 x) {
    uint128 r = reverseBits128(x);
    uint128 d = x ^ r;
    return (d == 0) ? 1 : 0;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128-bit values without hardware helpers.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit representation as hi/lo
- Leading-zero counting
- Branching vs branchless approaches
- Correct handling of zero input

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo helper assuming a 64-bit lz routine exists as lz64
static int lz64(uint64_t v){
  int n = 0;
  if ((v & 0xFFFFFFFF00000000ULL) == 0) { n += 32; v <<= 32; }
  if ((v & 0xFFFF000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 16; v <<= 16; }
  if ((v & 0xFF00000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 8; v <<= 8; }
  if ((v & 0xF000000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 4; v <<= 4; }
  if ((v & 0xC000000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 2; v <<= 2; }
  if ((v & 0x8000000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 1; }
  return n;
}
int countLeadingZeros128(uint128 x){
  if (x.hi != 0) return lz64(x.hi);
  if (x.lo != 0) return 64 + lz64(x.lo);
  return 128;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement countTrailingZeros128 without 128-bit intrinsics?
- How would you adapt this for a language without unsigned 64-bit types?

