void reverse128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo);

The reversal should map bit i to bit 127 - i, using only bitwise operations and shifts (no loops or conditionals). Include a brief justification and 1-2 quick test examples?

questions.explanation:
## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about multi-word bit manipulations and safe 64→128-bit extension without loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit reversal via two 64-bit reversals and swapping halves
- Parallel-bits reversal (mask/shifts)
- Endianness-insensitive mapping of bit indices

## Code Example
```javascript
function reverse64(x) {
  x = (x >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n | (x & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n;
  x = (x >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n | (x & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n;
  x = (x >> 4n) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) << 4n;
  x = (x >> 8n) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) << 8n;
  x = (x >> 16n) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) << 16n;
  x = (x >> 32n) | (x << 32n);
  return x;
}
function reverse128(hi, lo) {
  const revLo = reverse64(lo);
  const revHi = reverse64(hi);
  return { hi: revLo, lo: revHi };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to streaming 128-bit values?
- How to verify correctness with random tests?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses non-branching bit extraction (compression-like utility) crucial in low-latency paths. It tests SWAR techniques, mask reasoning, and edge-case handling for arbitrary masks.\n\n## Key Concepts
- SWAR bit-twiddling for packing bits\n- Mask-driven selection and order preservation\n- Handling 64-bit boundaries without loops\n\n## Code Example
```c
uint64_t compressBits64(uint64_t x, uint64_t mask) {
  x &= mask;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 1)) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 2)) & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 8)) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 32)) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL;
  return x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 128-bit inputs?\n- What are the performance implications on modern CPUs with this fixed-sequence approach?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of deeper bit-manipulation: 3D Morton ordering for spatial indexing, with no loops and tight constants.

## Key Concepts
- Morton (z-order) encoding across 3 dimensions
- bit spreading (part1by2) with masks/shifts
- avoiding branches for tight hot-paths

## Code Example
```javascript
function part1by2(n){
  let x = BigInt(n) & 0x1fffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 32n)) & 0x1f00000000ffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 16n)) & 0x1f0000ff0000ffn;
  x = (x | (x << 8n))  & 0x100f00f00f00f00fn;
  x = (x | (x << 4n))  & 0x10c30c30c30c30c3n;
  x = (x | (x << 2n))  & 0x1249249249249249n;
  return x;
}
function morton3D(x,y,z){
  return part1by2(x) | (part1by2(y) << 1n) | (part1by2(z) << 2n);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to 32-bit inputs? Trade-offs?
- Could you implement a inverse decode that recovers x,y,z from the code?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to implement a constant-time bit reversal to verify a binary palindrome using only bitwise ops.

## Key Concepts

- Palindrome property in binary
- Bit-reversal networks with masks
- Branch-free comparison

## Code Example

```javascript
// C-like implementation (illustrative)
int isBinaryPalindrome32(uint32_t n) {
  uint32_t x = n;
  x = ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555) | ((x & 0x55555555) << 1);
  x = ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333) | ((x & 0x33333333) << 2);
  x = ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F) | ((x & 0x0F0F0F0F) << 4);
  x = ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF) | ((x & 0x00FF00FF) << 8);
  x = (x >> 16) | (x << 16);
  return (x == n);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- Extend to 64-bit inputs; what changes? 
- Compare against a loop-based reversal for performance tests.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests familiarity with bit-slicing and fixed-mask patterns on multi-limb integers, not just 64-bit tricks. It also checks understanding of dividing work across 128-bit by treating halves independently. 

## Key Concepts
- Fixed masks for pairwise swaps (even/odd bits)
- 128-bit represented as two 64-bit lanes
- Branchless, constant-time bit permutation

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
uint128 swapAdjacentBits128(uint128 x){
  const uint64_t A = 0x5555555555555555ULL; // even bits
  const uint64_t B = 0xAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAULL; // odd bits
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = ((x.lo & A) << 1) | ((x.lo & B) >> 1);
  r.hi = ((x.hi & A) << 1) | ((x.hi & B) >> 1);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to swap bits in 3-bit groups across 192-bit? 
- What changes if you must preserve performance across SIMD-like widths with additional limbs?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply known bit-hack patterns to solve a common problem without loops or libs. Shows understanding of data-parallel aggregation and portability across compilers.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) counting
- Bit masks and shifts to sum bits in chunks
- Portability across 32-bit integers

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdint.h>
uint32_t countBits32(uint32_t x) {
  x = (x & 0x55555555) + ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F) + ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F);
  x = (x & 0x00FF00FF) + ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF);
  x = (x & 0x0000FFFF) + ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF);
  return x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for 64-bit integers?
- What are alternative methods and trade-offs (lookup-table vs SWAR)?
